# Wireshark is deliberately NOT declared. The `wireshark-app` cask (the GUI; the
# `wireshark` formula is the CLI-only build) installs two pkgs besides the app:
# "Add Wireshark to the system path" and "Install ChmodBPF", the latter
# a ROOT LaunchDaemon that chmods /dev/bpf* at every boot so non-root processes can
# capture. That is a persistent, admin-level change to how the machine handles packet
# capture, and it is not something a new Mac should acquire silently. Wireshark is an
# on-demand tool here: install it by hand when a capture is actually needed
#   brew install --cask wireshark-app
# or better, capture inside a container on a lab node and leave the Mac untouched.
# A hand install is not pinned: when its cask moves, a bare `brew upgrade` in `up`
# upgrades it too and stops at a password prompt (its two packages reinstall as
# root), so remove it after the capture:
#   brew uninstall --cask wireshark-app
# The uninstall leaves the `access_bpf` group. Leave it too: some capture tools ship
# their own copy of ChmodBPF, which gives /dev/bpf* to that same group at boot.

